## Requirements

- Svelte 5, React 19, or Vue 3.5
- A browser and execution context with WebGPU available

Handle WebGPU initialization failures through `onError`; availability depends on the browser, platform, GPU, and security context.

## Install

<InstallationTabs />

Only the adapter you import needs its peer dependencies:

- `svelte ^5`
- `react ^19` and `react-dom ^19`
- `vue ^3.5`

## Mount a material

Create the material outside the component render path and pass it to the adapter-specific `FragCanvas`:

```ts
import { defineMaterial } from 'spektral';

const material = defineMaterial({
	fragment: `
fn frag(uv: vec2f) -> vec4f {
	return vec4f(uv, 0.5, 1.0);
}
`
});
```

<FrameworkBlock
svelteCode={`<script lang="ts">\n  import { FragCanvas } from 'spektral/svelte';\n<\/script>\n\n<div class="canvas-host">\n  <FragCanvas {material} />\n</div>`}
reactCode={`import { FragCanvas } from 'spektral/react';\n\nexport function App() {\n  return <div className="canvas-host"><FragCanvas material={material} /></div>;\n}`}
vueCode={`<script setup lang="ts">\nimport { FragCanvas } from 'spektral/vue';\n<\/script>\n\n<template>\n  <div class="canvas-host"><FragCanvas :material="material" /></div>\n</template>`}
svelteLang="svelte"
reactLang="tsx"
vueLang="vue"
/>

`FragCanvas` fills its container. CSS controls its visible size; Spektral sets the backing-store resolution from the measured size and `dpr`.

## Add runtime updates

Call `useFrame`, `useSpektral`, `usePointer`, and `useTexture` from a descendant of `FragCanvas`. Declare resource names in `defineMaterial(...)` before updating them at runtime.

```ts
useFrame((state) => {
	state.setUniform('uTime', state.time);
});
```

The [Frame Scheduler](/docs/frame-scheduler) documents registration and invalidation. The [playground](/playground/diamond?framework=svelte) contains complete, editable applications for every adapter.

## Production baseline

- Treat material, pass, and compute entrypoint signatures as strict contracts.
- Use runtime setters for values; create a new material when the shader or resource layout changes.
- Choose `always`, `on-demand`, or `manual` rendering deliberately.
- Provide `onError` and decide whether to keep, disable, or replace the built-in overlay.
